EXT. SALT-FLAT FERRY LANDING - NOON

White. Everything white. Heat shimmer. A rotting dock jutting into a lake that dried up a decade ago. A ferry sits stranded on cracked crust, hull tilted.

DEACON CROWE, forties, sits on an upturned bucket by the ramp. Black coat, grey with salt. Rifle across his knees. He doesn't aim it. He just keeps it.

Hoofbeats, slow. A shape resolves in the glare. Rider. Mule.

BIRDIE PRUETT, thirties, wide hat, rolled sleeves. Sunburnt nose. She dismounts without hurry. Ties the mule to a piling.

He watches. Doesn't rise.

BIRDIE
Deacon.

DEACON
Birdie.

She takes a canteen off the saddle. Drinks. Holds it out. He considers it. Takes it.

BIRDIE
You're a long way from anybody.

DEACON
That's the idea.

She sits on the ramp's edge. Boots dangling over dry crust. He caps the canteen, passes it back. Neither says anything for a while.

A gull cries. Where it came from, no one knows. Both look up. Both look down again.

DEACON (CONT'D)
They say the lake'll come back.

BIRDIE
They say a lot.

He grins. Small. It leaves quick.

She pulls a folded paper from her vest. Turns it over in her fingers. He sees it. Eyes narrow. Rifle shifts a half inch on his knees.

They both go still. Wind hushes the dock timbers.

DEACON (CONT'D)
That mine?

BIRDIE
Was yours. It's a telegram now.

DEACON
Who from?

BIRDIE
Your sister. Says come home.

DEACON
(flat)
Don't have one.

BIRDIE
Then somebody's lying, Deacon.

He stares at the ferry. Her eyes stay on him. The heat hums.

She unfolds the paper. Holds it up between them like a card she doesn't want to play.

BIRDIE (CONT'D)
Read it with me.

He doesn't take it. He doesn't leave.

After a long moment he sets the rifle down on the planks. Slides over on the bucket. She shifts toward him, paper between them. They read.

DEACON
(low)
Hell.

BIRDIE
Yeah.

Salt glitters. Neither moves.
